{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788178040,"nonce":"ZWCnkDzvGZ1tg9/+rw78uqf5b6c=","message":"RECON LANDED — decompiled-source sweep for this spec, orchestrator session 6,\nBORGES. Full report goes to the worker at dispatch; the findings that change the\ncode are here. Every claim below was RE-VERIFIED by the orchestrator in the\ntree, not taken from the report.\n\n**THE LOAD-BEARING ANSWER: a trade CAN be transacted with no window on the\nstack.** `TradeSession` is a static class and\n`SetupWith` -\u003e `Tradeable.AdjustTo` -\u003e `UpdateCurrencyCount` -\u003e `TradeDeal\n.TryExecute` is fully window-free. So the verb shape stays \"transact the model\",\nexactly as this spec's invariant demands.\n\n**But TryExecute has an unguarded NRE on precisely the headless path**, and it\nwill crash the acceptance run if it is not handled. `RimWorld/TradeDeal.cs:163`,\nin the cannot-afford branch, verbatim:\n\n    Find.WindowStack.WindowOfType\u003cDialog_Trade\u003e().FlashSilver();\n\nWith no `Dialog_Trade` on the stack `WindowOfType\u003cT\u003e()` returns null and this\nNREs. This is the gate-lives-in-the-widget invariant in its purest form: the\ngame assumes its own UI is present and the model does not defend itself. The fix\nis NOT a try/catch — it is to make the branch unreachable by pre-validating\n`CurrencyTradeable != null \u0026\u0026 CurrencyTradeable.CountPostDealFor(Transactor\n.Colony) \u003e= 0` before calling, and returning the refusal ourselves with the\ngame's own \"MessageColonyCannotAfford\" string. Cite TradeDeal.cs:161-166.\nAlso: `TradeSession.Close()` has ZERO vanilla callers — the window used to own\nthe lifecycle, so this spec must close the session itself.\n\n**Clicking an option is reproducible without reflection.** `DiaOption.Activate()`\nis `protected`, but everything it does is public: close-if-`resolveTree`, invoke\n`action`, then follow `linkLateBind`/`link` via `GotoNode`. Letter removal lives\ninside the option's own `action`, so replaying the action IS the dismissal.\n`DiaOption.text` and `Dialog_NodeTree.curNode` are protected and still need the\nfield-refs `interactions` already binds.\n\n**`Activate()` does NOT check `disabled` — the UI does, one line earlier.**\n`DiaOption.cs:93` gates the button with `Widgets.ButtonText(..., !disabled, ...)`\nand only then calls `Activate()`. So a verb that replays Activate faithfully will\npress greyed-out buttons that no player can press. **Check `disabled` yourself\nand refuse with the game's own `disabledReason`.** This is the textbook case for\nDESIGN's widget-gate invariant: the gate is literally an argument to a Widgets\ncall.\n\n**Safe dismissal is `WindowStack.TryRemove(window, false)`** — it copies to temp\nlists, so mid-frame removal is by design. Never `Notify_PressedCancel` /\n`OnCancelKeyPressed` from tick context, and note node-tree dialogs set\n`closeOnCancel = false`, so \"escape\" is not what clears them.\n\n**Exactly three letter classes break the options-by-label contract**, plus one\nthat overrides and keeps it — the full `override ... OpenLetter` set is five:\n- `NewQuestLetter` — opens the Quests tab and removes itself; no accept option.\n- `ChoiceLetter_GrowthMoment` — extends LetterWithTimeout and has NO `Choices` at\n  all; the verb is its public `MakeChoices` plus `RemoveLetter`.\n- `BundleLetter` — FloatMenu, and it touches `Event.current` (NRE outside OnGUI).\n- `DeathLetter` overrides but keeps the contract.\nSo quest accept is its own verb, as the session-4 amendment said:\n`QuestPart_Choice.Choose(choice)` then `Quest.Accept(pawnOrNull)`, both public,\ngated by `QuestUtility.CanAcceptQuest` / `CanPawnAcceptQuest`. Accepting with two\nor more choices outstanding LOGS AN ERROR and auto-picks the first — a red error\nis an invariant breach, so choose before accepting, always.\n\n**`LetterStack.OpenAutomaticLetters`' early return is confirmed** (LetterStack.cs\n:140-143), and timed-out letters are reaped by the frame-based\n`LetterStackUpdate`. So the 1.7 wedge reproduces itself any time ticks advance\nwith a forcePause window up — which is exactly what this spec exists to prevent.\n\nTWO NEW WRITE-ON-READ HAZARDS, both verified end to end, neither touched by any\nshipped file (checked: no reference to either in Source/AutoRimmer/ — these are\nahead of us, not behind us):\n\n1. **`LetterStack.BundleLetter` (getter) burns a SCRIBED letter ID.** The getter\n   lazily does `LetterMaker.MakeLetter(LetterDefOf.BundleLetter)`\n   (LetterStack.cs:32-42), `MakeLetter` assigns\n   `obj.ID = Find.UniqueIDsManager.GetNextLetterID()` (LetterMaker.cs:13), and\n   `nextLetterID` is `Scribe_Values.Look`-scribed\n   (RimWorld/UniqueIDsManager.cs:350). So merely ASKING the stack for its bundle\n   letter permanently advances a scribed counter. Same class as\n   ResearchManager.GetProgress. Read the private `bundleLetterCache` field\n   instead, and report null as null.\n2. **`Settlement_TraderTracker.StockListForReading` regenerates an entire trader\n   inventory from a property getter** — and this is the most severe instance of\n   the class found in this project so far. When `stock == null ||\n   InnerListForReading.Empty()` it calls `RegenerateStock()`\n   (Settlement_TraderTracker.cs:27-37), which `TryDestroyStock()`s, builds a new\n   `ThingOwner`, sets the scribed `everGeneratedStock = true`, generates contents\n   through `ThingSetMakerDefOf.TraderStock.root.Generate(parms)` (RNG), and\n   `Find.WorldPawns.PassToWorld(pawn)`s any generated pawns (:265+). Zone.Cells\n   only shuffled a list; this one manufactures world state. **Scope note that\n   keeps it in proportion:** this is the WORLD-MAP settlement trader, and DESIGN's\n   non-goals rule out caravans and settlements for v1, so this spec's traders are\n   map/orbital ones through `TradeSession`. Do not reach for it, and do not let a\n   convenience helper wander onto it.\n\nLower-severity, same family, worth knowing: `Tradeable`'s price getters lazily\ncache off the STATIC session and NRE when no session is active, and\n`ChoiceLetter_GrowthMoment`'s open path rolls the shared `Rand` into scribed\nfields. All `Choices` iterators were checked and are enumeration-safe.","files":null}]}